In this lesson, we'll look at absolute and relative error. Every measurement you take is really just an approximation, so there's always some uncertainty involved. To describe that uncertainty, we use two different ideas. First up is absolute error. The key thing to know here is that absolute error has the same units as whatever you measured. So if you measured a length in metres, your absolute error is also in metres. It's just the size of your uncertainty, expressed in the same units as the original measurement. Then we have relative error. Relative error is different because it doesn't have any units at all. It's a pure number, a fraction. You get it by comparing the absolute error to the size of the measurement itself. Because it's a fraction, it's really handy when you want to compare the quality of measurements that were made in different units, or on very different scales. So think of absolute error as telling you how big the uncertainty is, and relative error as telling you how big it is compared to the measurement. Now let's see it in action.